ProsperoSunshine: pace large video frames to clients over the internet instead of sending each frame as one burst

The video broadcast thread sent every frame at once, at up to 800 Mbit/s for all sessions together. A keyframe of a slow
stream (100 to 200 kB at 3 Mbit/s) left the console in about 2 ms, and a path that keeps only the first part of such a
burst lost the rest every time; issue 37's logs show a remote path keeping exactly the first 39 packets of every larger
frame, so every keyframe was lost and the client asked for the next one.

Each frame is now prepared (FEC, headers, encryption) as it arrives and held until its packets are due. For a client
over the internet (Moonlight's smaller packets) the session's pacer (video/frame_pacing.hpp, with its reasons and
numbers) lets a frame up to a burst of about four average frames, at most 32 kB, go at once, as before, and spreads the
rest of a larger one at four times the stream's rate, never more than 100 ms after it arrived. A client on the LAN gets
every frame at once, as before. The thread sends whichever session's next packet is due first, so one session's pacing
doesn't hold up another's frames; a session's frames keep their order. Sunshine's own limit of about 800 Mbit/s over all
sessions stays (WireLimiter). Frames for a client whose port isn't known yet (before its first ping) are dropped instead
of sent to port 0. Audio has its own thread and isn't touched.

Frames point at their session from the capture on, in the video queue and while they are paced. The PS5 capture puts a
session::video_frame_ref_t into every packet it queues; the session counts them, a session that stops has its held
frames dropped, and session::join() waits until none is left before the session can be freed. Before, a frame still in
the queue when join() returned pointed at freed memory.

